---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

const title = "Free ADHD Test Online: Adult ADHD Screener (WHO ASRS v1.1) | FreeIQExam";;
const description = "Screen for adult ADHD symptoms with our free WHO ASRS v1.1 questionnaire. Evaluate inattentive and hyperactive patterns plus optional Go/No-Go impulse benchmark.";;
const canonicalUrl = 'https://freeiqexam.com/adhd-test';

const alternates = {
  // id: '/id/tes-adhd',
  // pt: '/pt/teste-tdah',
  // de: '/de/adhs-test-erwachsene',
  // fr: '/fr/test-tdah-adulte',
  // fi: '/fi/adhd-testi',
  // pl: '/pl/test-adhd',
};

const faqItems = [
  ['How accurate is an online adult ADHD screening test?', 'An online screener can identify symptom patterns that may justify a fuller assessment, but it cannot diagnose ADHD by itself. A clinician must consider persistence, impairment, developmental history, context, and alternative explanations.'],
  ['What is the difference between ADD and ADHD?', 'ADD is an older term. Current diagnostic language uses ADHD and describes predominantly inattentive, predominantly hyperactive/impulsive, or combined presentations. People who say ADD are often referring to the inattentive presentation.'],
  ['Why can adult ADHD present differently in women?', 'Inattentive symptoms, internal restlessness, perfectionistic compensation, and social masking can make symptoms less obvious. Hormonal transitions can also change attention, sleep, and symptom burden, but they are not specific to ADHD.'],
  ['What are the six questions in the WHO ASRS Part A screener?', 'Part A covers finishing project details, organizing tasks, remembering appointments or obligations, delaying effortful tasks, fidgeting while seated, and feeling overly active or driven by a motor.'],
  ['Can you develop ADHD as an adult if you did not have it as a child?', 'A browser screener cannot answer that definitively. ADHD is a neurodevelopmental condition, so a formal assessment looks for evidence of symptoms earlier in life while also considering other explanations for new adult-onset concentration problems.'],
  ['How does ADHD differ from chronic anxiety or depression?', 'Anxiety and depression can both impair concentration and executive functioning. ADHD can also overlap with them, and more than one condition can coexist. Timeline, triggers, impairment, sleep, mood, and developmental history help distinguish the patterns.'],
  ['What should I do if my screener indicates a high likelihood of ADHD?', 'Use the result as a structured conversation starter. Record real examples, frequency, functional effects, childhood history if available, sleep and mood factors, medications, and other relevant context, then discuss them with an appropriately qualified clinician.'],
  ['Does an online screening test count as a formal medical diagnosis?', 'No. It produces screening information only. A formal diagnosis requires a broader clinical evaluation. The optional Go/No-Go task is a performance benchmark and is not a standalone diagnostic test.']
];

const schemas = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
    '@id': `${canonicalUrl}#medical-page`,
    name: 'Adult ADHD Self-Report Scale (ASRS v1.1) Screener',
    url: canonicalUrl,
    medicalAudience: 'Patient',
    aspect: 'Diagnostic',
    isAccessibleForFree: true,
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebApplication',
    '@id': `${canonicalUrl}#application`,
    name: 'Free Adult ADHD Screening Questionnaire',
    url: canonicalUrl,
    applicationCategory: 'HealthApplication',
    operatingSystem: 'Any',
    isAccessibleForFree: true,
    offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
    accessibilitySummary: 'Keyboard-operable response controls, visible focus states, semantic headings, live announcements, and printable results.',
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://freeiqexam.com/' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Assessments', item: 'https://freeiqexam.com/test' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Adult ADHD Test', item: canonicalUrl },
    ],
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Quiz',
    '@id': `${canonicalUrl}#quiz`,
    name: 'Adult ADHD Screening Questionnaire — 18 Items',
    url: canonicalUrl,
    numberOfQuestions: 18,
    isAccessibleForFree: true,
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    '@id': `${canonicalUrl}#faq`,
    mainEntity: faqItems.map(([name, text]) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text },
    })),
  },
];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl} alternates={alternates} schema={schemas}>
  <Header />

  <main id="assessment-page-content" class="flex-1 bg-white text-zinc-950 dark:bg-black dark:text-white">
    <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-6xl px-4 py-10 sm:px-6 sm:py-14 lg:px-8 lg:py-20">
      <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="mb-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-sm font-medium text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
        <a href="/" class="hover:text-zinc-950 dark:hover:text-white">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
        <a href="/test" class="hover:text-zinc-950 dark:hover:text-white">Assessments</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
        <span aria-current="page" class="text-zinc-950 dark:text-white">Adult ADHD Test</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="mx-auto max-w-4xl text-center">
        <div class="inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-violet-200 bg-violet-50 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-violet-700 dark:border-violet-900/60 dark:bg-violet-950/40 dark:text-violet-300 sm:text-sm">
          WHO ASRS v1.1 · Clinical Psychometrics
        </div>
        <h1 class="font-display font-extrabold text-3xl sm:text-5xl md:text-6xl text-zinc-900 dark:text-white tracking-[-0.025em] mb-4">
        Adult ADHD Test (WHO ASRS v1.1 Clinical Screener)
      </h1>
        <p class="mx-auto mt-6 max-w-3xl text-base leading-relaxed text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 sm:text-lg">
          Take a free adult ADHD screening questionnaire based on the supplied WHO ASRS v1.1 framework. Review Part A screening status, inattentive and hyperactive/impulsive symptom patterns, and an optional Go/No-Go response-control benchmark without an email gate.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3 text-sm sm:text-base">
          {['18 questions', 'Five response options', 'Printable summary', 'No account required'].map((item) => (
            <span class="rounded-full border border-zinc-200 px-4 py-2.5 text-zinc-700 dark:border-zinc-800 dark:text-zinc-300">{item}</span>
          ))}
        </div>
      </header>

      <section id="adhd-app" class="mx-auto mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <div class="rounded-[2rem] border border-zinc-200 bg-zinc-50/80 p-5 dark:border-zinc-800 dark:bg-zinc-950/70 sm:p-8">
          <div id="assessment-intro" class="rounded-[1.5rem] border border-violet-200 bg-violet-50 p-6 dark:border-violet-900/50 dark:bg-violet-950/30 sm:p-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-violet-700 dark:text-violet-300 sm:text-sm">Before you begin</p>
            <h2 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">Screening is not diagnosis</h2>
            <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300 sm:text-lg">
              Answer based on your typical experience. The screen estimates symptom endorsement against the thresholds used by this implementation. It cannot confirm ADHD and should not replace a medical or psychological assessment.
            </p>
            <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
              <button id="begin-assessment" type="button" class="rounded-full bg-violet-600 px-7 py-4 text-base font-semibold text-white transition hover:scale-[1.02] active:scale-[0.98] focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-violet-500">Start adult ADHD screener</button>
              <span class="self-center text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 sm:text-base">Keyboard: 1–5 to answer · ← / → to navigate</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <section id="questionnaire-section" class="hidden" aria-labelledby="question-heading">
            <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 sm:flex-row sm:items-end sm:justify-between">
              <div>
                <p id="question-part" class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-violet-700 dark:text-violet-300 sm:text-sm">Part A · Core Clinical Screener</p>
                <p id="question-domain" class="mt-2 text-base font-semibold text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Inattention</p>
              </div>
              <div class="text-left sm:text-right">
                <div id="question-number" class="text-base font-semibold sm:text-lg">Question 1 of 18</div>
                <div id="progress-label" class="mt-1 text-sm text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400 sm:text-base">0% answered</div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="mt-5 h-2 overflow-hidden rounded-full bg-zinc-200 dark:bg-zinc-800"><div id="progress-fill" class="h-full rounded-full bg-violet-600 transition-all duration-300" style="width:4%"></div></div>
            <article class="mt-8 rounded-[1.75rem] border border-zinc-200 bg-white p-6 dark:border-zinc-800 dark:bg-zinc-950 sm:p-9">
              <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400 sm:text-base">
                <span class="rounded-full border border-zinc-200 px-3 py-1.5 dark:border-zinc-800">Choose one</span>
                <span class="rounded-full border border-zinc-200 px-3 py-1.5 dark:border-zinc-800">Never → Very Often</span>
              </div>
              <h2 id="question-heading" class="mt-6 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl"></h2>
              <p id="question-context" class="mt-3 text-base text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400 sm:text-lg"></p>
              <div id="likert-options" class="mt-8 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-5" role="group" aria-label="Frequency response options"></div>
              <p class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-violet-200 bg-violet-50/70 px-5 py-4 text-sm text-violet-900 dark:border-violet-900/50 dark:bg-violet-950/30 dark:text-violet-200 sm:text-base">Highlighted options are the responses counted as shaded for the current item.</p>
              <div class="mt-8 flex flex-col-reverse gap-3 sm:flex-row sm:justify-between">
                <button id="back-button" type="button" class="rounded-full border border-zinc-300 bg-white px-6 py-3.5 text-base font-semibold dark:border-zinc-700 dark:bg-zinc-900">Back</button>
                <button id="next-button" type="button" class="rounded-full bg-zinc-950 px-7 py-3.5 text-base font-semibold text-white disabled:cursor-not-allowed disabled:opacity-40 dark:bg-white dark:text-zinc-950">Next question</button>
              </div>
            </article>
          </section>

          <section id="cognitive-section" class="hidden" aria-labelledby="cognitive-title">
            <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 sm:flex-row sm:items-end sm:justify-between">
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-violet-700 dark:text-violet-300 sm:text-sm">Optional objective section</p>
                <h2 id="cognitive-title" class="mt-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">20-Trial Go/No-Go impulse benchmark</h2>
              </div>
              <div class="text-sm text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400 sm:text-base">Session <span id="cognitive-session-id" class="font-mono">—</span></div>
            </div>
            <div class="mt-6 rounded-[1.75rem] border border-zinc-200 bg-white p-6 dark:border-zinc-800 dark:bg-zinc-950 sm:p-9">
              <div id="cognitive-start-panel">
                <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 sm:text-lg">Green means <strong>press Space or tap</strong>. Red means <strong>withhold</strong>. The task records valid Go reaction times and No-Go commission errors. It is a performance benchmark, not a diagnostic test.</p>
                <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
                  <button id="begin-cognitive" type="button" class="rounded-full bg-violet-600 px-7 py-4 text-base font-semibold text-white transition hover:scale-[1.02] active:scale-[0.98]">Begin 20-trial challenge</button>
                  <button id="skip-cognitive" type="button" class="rounded-full border border-zinc-300 px-6 py-4 text-base font-semibold dark:border-zinc-700">Skip and view questionnaire results</button>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div id="cognitive-countdown" class="hidden py-5 text-center text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-violet-600 dark:text-violet-400" aria-live="polite">3</div>
              <div class="mt-7 flex items-center justify-between text-sm text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400 sm:text-base"><span>Trial</span><span id="cognitive-trial">0 / 20</span></div>
              <div class="mt-2 h-2 overflow-hidden rounded-full bg-zinc-200 dark:bg-zinc-800"><div id="cognitive-progress-fill" class="h-full rounded-full bg-violet-600" style="width:0%"></div></div>
              <button id="cognitive-target" type="button" class="mx-auto mt-10 flex aspect-square w-full max-w-[22rem] items-center justify-center rounded-full border-2 border-zinc-300 bg-zinc-100 opacity-0 transition-transform duration-75 focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-4 focus-visible:ring-violet-500 dark:border-zinc-700 dark:bg-zinc-900" aria-label="Go/No-Go response target">
                <span id="cognitive-target-label" class="text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">Wait…</span>
              </button>
              <p class="mt-5 text-center text-sm text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400 sm:text-base">Press <kbd class="rounded border border-zinc-300 bg-zinc-100 px-2 py-1 font-mono dark:border-zinc-700 dark:bg-zinc-900">Space</kbd> or tap the circle.</p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section id="results-section" class="hidden" aria-labelledby="results-title">
            <div class="rounded-[1.75rem] border border-zinc-200 bg-white p-6 dark:border-zinc-800 dark:bg-zinc-950 sm:p-9">
              <div class="flex flex-col gap-5 sm:flex-row sm:items-start sm:justify-between">
                <div>
                  <div id="result-badge" class="result-badge low">Low clinical likelihood</div>
                  <h2 id="results-title" class="mt-5 text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">Your screening profile</h2>
                  <p id="result-classification" class="mt-2 text-xl font-bold text-violet-700 dark:text-violet-300">Low / sub-clinical pattern</p>
                </div>
                <div class="rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 bg-zinc-50 px-4 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-600 dark:border-zinc-800 dark:bg-zinc-900 dark:text-zinc-300 sm:text-base">Not a diagnosis</div>
              </div>
              <p id="result-narrative" class="mt-6 text-base leading-relaxed text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 sm:text-lg"></p>

              <div class="mt-8 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
                <article class="score-card"><div class="score-label">Part A</div><div id="part-a-score" class="mt-3 text-3xl font-extrabold">0 / 6</div><div id="part-a-status" class="score-status">Below threshold</div><div class="meter"><span id="part-a-bar"></span></div></article>
                <article class="score-card"><div class="score-label">Inattention</div><div id="inattention-score" class="mt-3 text-3xl font-extrabold">0 / 36</div><div id="inattention-status" class="score-status">Lower endorsement</div><div class="meter"><span id="inattention-bar"></span></div></article>
                <article class="score-card"><div class="score-label">Hyperactivity / Impulsivity</div><div id="hyperactivity-score" class="mt-3 text-3xl font-extrabold">0 / 36</div><div id="hyperactivity-status" class="score-status">Lower endorsement</div><div class="meter"><span id="hyperactivity-bar"></span></div></article>
              </div>

              <article id="cognitive-result-card" class="mt-4 hidden rounded-3xl border border-zinc-200 bg-zinc-50 p-6 dark:border-zinc-800 dark:bg-zinc-900">
                <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400 sm:text-base">Go / No-Go benchmark</p>
                <div class="mt-5 grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 sm:grid-cols-4">
                  <div class="stat-card"><span>Mean RT</span><strong id="cognitive-rt">—</strong></div>
                  <div class="stat-card"><span>RT variability</span><strong id="cognitive-rtv">—</strong></div>
                  <div class="stat-card"><span>Commission errors</span><strong id="cognitive-errors">—</strong></div>
                  <div class="stat-card"><span>Omissions</span><strong id="cognitive-omissions">—</strong></div>
                </div>
              </article>

              <article class="mt-6 rounded-3xl border border-zinc-200 p-6 dark:border-zinc-800">
                <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400 sm:text-base">Executive function matrix</p>
                <h3 class="mt-2 text-xl font-bold sm:text-2xl">Symptom-domain snapshot</h3>
                <div id="profile-matrix" class="mt-6 space-y-5"></div>
              </article>

              <div class="mt-8 flex flex-col gap-3 sm:flex-row sm:flex-wrap">
                <button id="print-result" type="button" class="rounded-full bg-zinc-950 px-7 py-4 text-base font-semibold text-white dark:bg-white dark:text-zinc-950">Print Clinician Intake Summary</button>
                <button id="share-result" type="button" class="rounded-full border border-zinc-300 px-6 py-4 text-base font-semibold dark:border-zinc-700">Share screening summary</button>
                <button id="retake-result" type="button" class="rounded-full border border-zinc-300 px-6 py-4 text-base font-semibold dark:border-zinc-700">Retake assessment</button>
              </div>

              <div class="mt-8 rounded-3xl border border-violet-200 bg-violet-50 p-6 dark:border-violet-900/50 dark:bg-violet-950/30">
                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold sm:text-2xl">Read the result in context</h3>
                <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-relaxed text-violet-950 dark:text-violet-100 sm:text-lg">A positive screen means the response pattern crossed this screener's threshold. It does not establish ADHD, severity, presentation, or treatment need. Sleep disorders, anxiety, depression, bipolar-spectrum symptoms, medication effects, stress, and other conditions can overlap with attention and executive-function complaints.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </section>

          <p id="adhd-live-region" class="sr-only" aria-live="polite"></p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <article class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-4xl text-base leading-relaxed text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300 sm:text-lg">
        <section><p class="eyebrow">Clinical guide</p><h2 class="mt-3 section-title">Understanding adult ADHD beyond a single score</h2><p class="mt-6">Adult ADHD is best understood as a persistent pattern of attention-regulation, inhibition, organization, and executive-function difficulties that creates meaningful problems in everyday life. A questionnaire helps organize symptoms, but diagnosis depends on the broader story: how long the pattern has existed, how consistently it appears, how much it interferes with functioning, and what other explanations might account for the same experiences.</p></section>

        <section class="guide-section"><h2 class="section-title">1. The neurobiology of adult ADHD</h2><p class="mt-5">ADHD is associated with differences across distributed brain systems involved in attention, motivation, timing, action selection, and executive control. Frontostriatal networks are often discussed because they support planning, response selection, reward processing, and goal maintenance. Dopamine and norepinephrine both participate in these networks. It is more accurate to think of ADHD as a problem of regulation across systems than as a simple shortage of a single neurotransmitter.</p><p class="mt-5">The dorsolateral prefrontal cortex contributes to working memory, planning, inhibition, and maintaining goals while distractions compete for attention. A person can therefore know what needs to happen while still struggling to sustain the mental state required to start or finish it. This explains why intelligence and educational attainment do not protect someone from executive dysfunction.</p><p class="mt-5">The Default Mode Network is active during internally directed thought. Effective task performance requires flexible coordination between internally focused and task-focused networks. Difficulties suppressing or switching from spontaneous internal thought can feel like mind-wandering, intrusive ideas, or an inability to remain mentally attached to low-interest work. These ideas are useful for understanding research, not for diagnosing an individual from a browser test.</p></section>

        <section class="guide-section"><h2 class="section-title">2. DSM-5 diagnostic criteria and what screening leaves out</h2><p class="mt-5">A screening questionnaire samples symptoms; a diagnosis is a clinical formulation. A full adult ADHD assessment considers persistent symptoms of inattention and/or hyperactivity-impulsivity, clinically meaningful impairment, evidence that the pattern was present earlier in development, and whether another condition better explains it. Symptoms should not be interpreted from one unusually stressful week.</p><p class="mt-5">Functional impairment can appear as chronic lateness, missed deadlines, incomplete tasks, repeated administrative mistakes, losing important objects, excessive switching between tasks, unsafe impulsive decisions, or relationship strain caused by interruption and forgetfulness. Clinicians often seek examples across more than one setting because context matters. A strong performance in a highly structured environment does not automatically cancel difficulties elsewhere, especially when it depends on heavy compensatory effort.</p></section>

        <section class="guide-section"><h2 class="section-title">3. The three adult presentations</h2><h3 class="mt-8 text-xl font-bold sm:text-2xl">Predominantly inattentive</h3><p class="mt-4">This pattern centers on distractibility, disorganization, forgetfulness, losing things, difficulty sustaining attention, and trouble following through. Adults may describe knowing exactly what to do but being unable to initiate a low-interest task until urgency becomes high. Time estimation, working-memory load, and switching between priorities can become major bottlenecks.</p><h3 class="mt-8 text-xl font-bold sm:text-2xl">Predominantly hyperactive/impulsive</h3><p class="mt-4">Adult hyperactivity can be internal rather than visibly dramatic. People may describe motor restlessness, difficulty sitting through long situations, difficulty relaxing, talking excessively, impatience, interrupting, or acting before a conversational or procedural turn has finished.</p><h3 class="mt-8 text-xl font-bold sm:text-2xl">Combined presentation</h3><p class="mt-4">Combined presentation involves clinically significant features from both domains. The experience can be paradoxical: someone may feel physically or mentally restless while simultaneously struggling to begin an important routine task. High-interest activities can still produce intense engagement, while boring but necessary activities remain difficult to regulate.</p></section>

        <section class="guide-section"><h2 class="section-title">4. Adult ADHD in women: masking and hormonal context</h2><p class="mt-5">The supplied research emphasizes inattentive masking, internal restlessness, compensatory perfectionism, and later recognition in women. A person may create elaborate reminder systems, over-prepare for routine tasks, recheck work repeatedly, or spend nights catching up after daytime attention drift. External performance can look strong while the internal cognitive cost is high.</p><p class="mt-5">Hormonal transitions can change sleep, mood, energy, and cognitive experience. Puberty, postpartum changes, and perimenopause may alter the burden of existing symptoms in some people. Estrogen interacts with dopaminergic systems, but hormonal change is not a diagnostic shortcut. Clinicians may also need to consider thyroid disease, anemia, medication effects, sleep disorders, depression, anxiety, and ordinary life stress.</p></section>

        <section class="guide-section"><h2 class="section-title">5. Differential diagnosis and comorbidities</h2><p class="mt-5">Anxiety can pull attention toward worry and threat monitoring. Depression can reduce concentration, energy, motivation, and pleasure. Bipolar-spectrum disorders involve episodic changes in mood, sleep, energy, and behavior rather than a stable lifelong executive pattern. Autism can overlap with executive function, sensory regulation, and social-communication differences. Sleep apnea and chronic sleep restriction can produce substantial daytime attention problems.</p><p class="mt-5">The goal is not to choose the label that sounds most familiar. The goal is to understand the full timeline, triggers, impairment, and co-occurring symptoms. More than one condition can be present simultaneously, and a good assessment considers that possibility rather than forcing every symptom into a single category.</p></section>

        <section class="guide-section"><h2 class="section-title">6. Evidence-based treatment pathways</h2><p class="mt-5">Treatment is individualized. Depending on the person's goals and clinical history, care may combine education, environmental changes, behavioral strategies, psychotherapy, and medication. Common prescription categories include stimulant medicines such as methylphenidate or amphetamine formulations and non-stimulant medicines such as atomoxetine or guanfacine. These medications have different benefits, risks, contraindications, and monitoring requirements and should be selected with an appropriately qualified prescriber.</p><p class="mt-5">CBT adapted for ADHD can target procrastination, planning, prioritization, emotional regulation, and follow-through. Externalizing working memory through visible calendars, reminders, checklists, and task breakdowns can reduce cognitive load. Workplace accommodations may include written instructions, predictable scheduling, reduced interruptions, or a quieter work area when appropriate and legally available.</p></section>

        <section class="guide-section"><h2 class="section-title">7. How to use this result constructively</h2><p class="mt-5">A screener is most useful as a structured conversation starter. Record a few concrete examples from work, school, home, finances, driving, and relationships. Note frequency, consequences, coping systems, and whether the difficulty is longstanding. Older school reports, family observations, or other historical records can sometimes help reconstruct childhood patterns that were never formally recognized.</p><p class="mt-5">The optional Go/No-Go task can provide an interesting snapshot of response inhibition and reaction-time consistency, but a normal or abnormal performance does not prove or disprove ADHD. Computerized tasks are sensitive to many factors including fatigue, practice, distraction, sleep, device latency, and motivation. A clinician should place any performance result inside the broader history.</p><p class="mt-5">If the questionnaire suggests a high likelihood and symptoms are causing real impairment, discuss the pattern with a primary-care clinician, psychiatrist, psychologist, or other qualified professional. If the result is low but your difficulties remain severe, that is also worth discussing; a low screen does not rule out other mental-health, medical, sleep, or environmental causes of cognitive difficulty.</p></section>

        <section id="faq" class="guide-section"><p class="eyebrow">FAQ</p><h2 class="section-title mt-3">Frequently asked questions about adult ADHD tests</h2><div class="mt-7 space-y-3">{faqItems.map(([question, answer]) => (<details class="group rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 bg-white px-5 py-5 dark:border-zinc-800 dark:bg-zinc-950"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-base font-semibold sm:text-lg"><span>{question}</span><span aria-hidden="true" class="transition group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-4 border-t border-zinc-200 pt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-zinc-600 dark:border-zinc-800 dark:text-zinc-300 sm:text-lg">{answer}</p></details>))}</div></section>

        <section class="guide-section rounded-[2rem] border border-zinc-200 bg-zinc-50 p-7 dark:border-zinc-800 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 sm:p-9"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold sm:text-3xl">Clinical disclaimer</h2><p class="mt-4">FreeIQExam provides educational and screening-oriented tools. This page is not a substitute for licensed clinical care, emergency services, or personalized medical advice. Do not start, stop, or change prescription treatment from an online score.</p><p class="mt-4">Crisis support: U.S. and Canada — call or text 988. U.K. and Ireland — Samaritans 116 123. Elsewhere, use the emergency or crisis resources available in your country.</p></section>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>

